[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Jobava London"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The Jobava London is named after the Georgian grandmaster Baadur Jobava. Lichess calls it the Rapport–Jobava System, after Jobava and the grandmaster Richárd Rapport. As in the London System, the dark-squared bishop comes out to f4 before the e-pawn moves and shuts it in. The difference is the queen's knight: it goes to c3, in front of the c-pawn. Each board below shows what the knight on c3 adds.}
*

[Chapter "Nb5 hits c7"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{From c3 the knight can jump to b5, where it hits c7 together with the f4 bishop. Nxc7+ would fork Black's king and the rook on a8, so Black has to answer at once. The usual answer, ...Na6, guards c7 but puts a knight on the edge of the board.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 {[%csl Yc7] The idea starts from this position.}
4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Knight and bishop both hit c7.} Na6 {[%cal Ga6c7] Guards c7. Six Black players in ten answer this way.}
5. e3 {[%cal Gf1a6] Opens the f1 bishop's diagonal, which runs to a6.} c6 {[%cal Gc6b5] Drives the knight back.}
6. Nc3 {The knight returns to c3. Black's knight on a6 still has a long way back to the center. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Bxa6 breaks Black's pawns"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{A knight on a6 has only one defender, the b7 pawn. If Black's bishop leaves f8 before the knight has gone back to c7, White can take it: Bxa6 bxa6 leaves Black with two pawns on the a-file, both of them weak. White gives up the bishop pair, but Black's queenside stays broken for the rest of the game.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Na6 5. e3 c6 6. Nc3 {[%csl Ya6] The idea starts from this position.}
6... Bd6 {[%cal Gd6f4] Offers a trade of the dark-squared bishops, but it leaves the knight on a6 to fend for itself.}
7. Bxa6 {[%cal Gf1a6] Takes the knight.} bxa6 {[%csl Ya6,Ya7] The only way to take back. Black now has two a-pawns.}
8. Na4 {[%cal Ga4c5] The knight heads for c5, the hole in front of the c6 pawn. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "h4 against a fianchetto"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{When Black fianchettoes the f8 bishop with ...g6 and ...Bg7 and castles short, White's h-pawn can march to h5 and hit the g6 pawn in front of Black's king. Once the pawns are traded on g6, the h-file is open for White's rook.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 g6 4. e3 Bg7 {[%csl Yg6] The idea starts from this position.}
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The h-pawn heads for h5.} O-O {Black castles into it.}
6. h5 {[%cal Gh5g6] Hits g6.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black hits back in the center.}
7. hxg6 hxg6 {[%cal Gh1h8] The h-file is open for White's rook. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans against the bishop on f5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Black's light-squared bishop often comes to f5: at once, or one move later, after a pawn move to a6 or c6. There it is active and watches d3, where White's own light-squared bishop wants to go. This course meets it the same way in every line: White's bishop goes to d3 anyway, to trade it off. The boards show what follows the trade and what to do if Black's bishop steps back to g6.}
*

[Chapter "Take back with the c-pawn"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{After ...Bxd3, take back with the c-pawn. The pawn on d3 guards e4 and c4, and the c-file opens for a rook. With the light-squared bishops gone, Black's b7 pawn has lost a defender, so Qb3 hits it at once.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Bd3 Bxd3 {The idea starts from this position.}
6. cxd3 {[%cal Gd3e4,Gd3c4,Ga1c1] The pawn guards e4 and c4, and the c-file is open for a rook.} Bd6 {A third of Black players offer a trade of the dark-squared bishops.}
7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] Hits b7, which the c8 bishop used to guard.} Qc8 {Guards b7.}
8. Bg3 {[%cal Gh1h3] If Black takes on g3, take back with the h-pawn: the h-file opens for White's rook. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "h4 against ...Bg6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{If Black keeps the bishop with ...Bg6, push the h-pawn. h4 threatens h5, which drives the bishop off the diagonal. Black's usual answer is to trade on d3 after all, and White's h-pawn has gained space for free.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 a6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Bd3 Bg6 {[%cal Gd3g6] The idea starts from this position.}
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5 and h6.} e6
7. h5 {[%cal Gh5g6] Hits the bishop.} Bxd3 {The bishop has nowhere better to go.}
8. cxd3 {[%cal Gh5h6] White has the same structure as after an early trade on d3, plus a pawn on h5 that cramps Black's kingside. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "f3 and g4 against ...Bg4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]

{When Black's bishop comes to g4 instead, it has no retreat to the queenside. White chases it with f3, g4 and h4, gaining space on the kingside with every move. The bishop ends up shut in on h7 or g6, where it hits only White's pawns.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c6 4. e3 Bg4 {[%csl Yg4] The idea starts from this position.}
5. f3 {[%cal Gf3g4] Hits the bishop.} Bh5
6. g4 {[%cal Gg4h5] Hits it again.} Bg6
7. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5, which would trap the bishop. Black has to make room with ...h6 or ...h5. Stockfish: +0.8, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the bishop goes to b4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{This course meets the Jobava with a pawn move to e6, the most common reply. The pawn guards d5 a second time and opens a diagonal for the dark-squared bishop. Then the bishop goes to b4 whenever White allows it, and the c-pawn moves up to hit White's center. The boards show why the bishop belongs on b4.}
*

[Chapter "...Bb4 pins White's best piece"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{The knight on c3 is what makes the Jobava work: it jumps to b5 and supports e4. ...Bb4 pins it to White's king. Then ...c5 hits d4. If White takes on c5, the knight on d7 takes back and hits the bishop on d3.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 {The idea starts from this position.}
4... Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3,Gc3e1] Pins the knight on c3.}
5. Bd3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits the center at once.}
6. dxc5 Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7c5] The knight takes back on c5 next.}
7. Nf3 Nxc5 {[%cal Gc5d3] The pawn is back, and the knight hits the bishop on d3. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "...Bxc3+ and ...Qa5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{When White plays a3 to ask the bishop, take on c3. White gets the bishop pair, but the pawn on c3 is doubled, and nothing guards it. ...c5 opens the diagonal for the queen, and ...Qa5 hits c3. At this level Black scores 77% after 6...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bb4 5. a3 {The idea starts from this position.}
5... Bxc3+ {[%cal Gb4c3] Takes the knight.}
6. bxc3 {[%csl Yc3,Yc2] White's c-pawns are doubled.} c5 {[%cal Gd8a5] Opens the way for the queen.}
7. Nf3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Hits c3. White has to give up a pawn or make an awkward move. Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Against Nb5: ...Bb4+ and ...Ba5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]

{Against 4.Nb5, check first. After 5.c3 the bishop drops back to a5, and the pawn on c3 has taken the square the knight would use to go home. Then ...a6 drives the knight to a3, on the edge of the board.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7] The idea starts from this position.}
4... Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check.}
5. c3 {Almost every White player blocks with the pawn.} Ba5 {[%cal Ga5e1] The bishop stays on the diagonal.}
6. a4 a6 {[%cal Ga6b5] Hits the knight.}
7. Na3 {[%csl Ya3] The knight has to go to the edge. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Side "both"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Both sides' pieces aim at the same few squares, c7, c3 and d5, so the same tricks come up again and again. Three of these boards show White's tricks and two show Black's. The trap chapters drill them.}
*

[Chapter "Nb5 and the fork on c7"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Side "white"]

{After 3...c5 4.e3, the natural 4...Nc6? leaves c7 guarded only by the queen. 5.Nb5! threatens Nc7+, forking king and rook. One Black player in three plays 4...Nc6, and White scores 69% after 5.Nb5 at this level.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6? {[%csl Yc7] The idea starts from this position.}
5. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Threatens Nc7+.} e5 {Almost six Black players in ten hit the bishop.}
6. dxe5 {[%cal Gb5c7] The threat is still there.} Ne4 {The most common reply.}
7. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5e4,Gb5c7] Wins a pawn and hits the knight on e4, and Nc7+ is still a threat. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Qxd5 when the queen is overloaded"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Side "white"]

{After 3...Nc6 4.Nb5 e5 5.dxe5, the knight on f6 is attacked. If it goes to e4, Black's queen is the only guard of d5. White takes the pawn: after 6...Qxd5, 7.Nxc7+ forks king and queen.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Nc6 4. Nb5 e5 5. dxe5 {The idea starts from this position.}
5... Ne4? {[%csl Yd5] One Black player in six. Now only the queen guards d5.}
6. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5e4,Gb5c7] Takes the pawn and hits the knight on e4.} Qxd5 {Takes the queen, but the knight has a fork.}
7. Nxc7+ {[%cal Gc7e8,Gc7d5] Check, and the queen on d5 is hit.} Kd8 8. Nxd5 {White is two pawns up. Stockfish: +3.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Bxg6 and Qd3 against ...h5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Side "white"]

{When Black answers h4 with ...h5, the h-pawn no longer guards g6. After Bxg6, Black must take back with the f-pawn, and that pawn on g6 has no guard. Qd3 hits it, and Qxg6+ follows. White scores 94% after 6...h5 at this level, in a small number of games.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Bd3 Bg6 6. h4 {The idea starts from this position.}
6... h5? {Stops h5, but g6 loses its guard.}
7. Bxg6 {[%cal Gd3g6] Takes the bishop.} fxg6 {[%csl Yg6] Forced: the h-pawn is on h5.}
8. Qd3 {[%cal Gd3g6] Hits g6. Qxg6+ comes next. Stockfish: +3.0, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "...Ne4 against Qd2"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "black"]
[Side "black"]

{When White's c-pawns are doubled and Black's queen hits c3, Qd2 looks like the natural guard. It isn't. ...Ne4 hits the queen, and when it moves, c3 falls with check. At this level Black scores 89% after 8.Qd2?, which three White players in ten choose.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bb4 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] The idea starts from this position.}
8. Qd2? {Guards c3 with the queen.} Ne4! {[%cal Ge4d2] Hits the queen.}
9. Qd3 c4! {[%cal Gc4d3] Drives the queen away from c3.}
10. Qd1 Qxc3+ {[%cal Gc3a1,Gc3e1] Check, and the rook on a1 is hit too. Stockfish: −3.7, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Don't retreat to b6 against a4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Idea "white"]
[Side "black"]

{After 4.Nb5 Bb4+ 5.c3 Ba5 6.a4, the bishop on a5 is short of squares. If it drops back to b6, the a-pawn traps it. One Black player in twenty-five goes wrong here, and White scores 91% from there. 6...a6 is the right move.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Bb4+ 5. c3 Ba5 6. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] The idea starts from this position.}
6... Bb6? {Steps off the a-file.}
7. a5 {[%cal Ga5b6] Hits the bishop. It has no safe square left.} Bxa5 {The best of a bad choice.}
8. Rxa5 {White has won a bishop for a pawn. Stockfish: +4.5, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Knight to b5: 3...e6 4.Nb5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{Almost one Black player in four answers 3.Bf4 with 3...e6. Jump into b5 at once: the knight and the f4 bishop both hit c7. Six Black players in ten guard c7 with 4...Na6. Play e3, and when ...c6 drives the knight back to c3, Black's knight on a6 is still far from the center. Against 4...Bd6 and 4...Bb4+, see Advanced variations.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6
4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Threatens Nxc7+, forking king and rook.} Na6 {Guards c7, but the knight stands on the edge of the board.}
5. e3 {[%cal Gf1a6] Opens the f1 bishop's diagonal, which runs to a6.} c6 {[%cal Gc6b5] Seven Black players in ten kick the knight. Against 5...Be7 or 5...Bd7, keep developing with Nf3 or c3.}
6. Nc3 {The knight comes back. It has moved three times, but Black's knight on a6 needs two more moves to reach the center.} Nc7 {[%cal Gc7e6,Gc7b5] The most common move, in almost four games in ten. Against 6...Bd6 or 6...Bb4, take on a6 (see Knight to b5: other fifth and sixth moves).}
7. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3h7] The bishop aims at h7. Next come Nf3 and castling. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Early bishop: 3...Bf5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{One Black player in five brings the bishop out at once. Develop with e3, then offer the trade with Bd3. If Black takes, take back with the c-pawn: it guards e4 and opens the c-file (see Key ideas: Plans against the bishop on f5). Three Black players in ten keep the bishop with 5...Bg6 instead: push the h-pawn (see Early bishop: 5...Bg6).}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Bf5
4. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop. 4.f3 is also popular, but Stockfish prefers 4.e3.} e6 {Two Black players in three. After 4...a6 or 4...c6, the position is the same as in Guarding b5: 3...a6 and Slav setup: 3...c6.}
5. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3f5] Offers the trade of light-squared bishops.} Bxd3 {Four Black players in ten take.}
6. cxd3 {[%cal Gd3e4,Ga1c1] Take back with the c-pawn. It guards e4, and the c-file opens for a rook.} Bd6 {A third of Black players offer a trade of the dark-squared bishops.}
7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] With the light-squared bishops gone, b7 has lost a defender.} Qc8 {Guards b7.}
8. Bg3 {If Black takes on g3, take back with the h-pawn, and the h-file opens for your rook.} O-O 9. Nf3 {[%cal Ga1c1] Next come Rc1 and castling. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Guarding b5: 3...a6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{About one Black player in six plays 3...a6, which takes b5 away from White's knight. Develop London style with e3, Bd3 and Nf3. Three Black players in ten then bring the bishop to f5: offer the trade with Bd3, as against 3...Bf5. Black's other fourth moves are in Advanced variations.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 a6
4. e3 {Seven White players in ten.} Bf5 {About three Black players in ten. Against 4...e6, 4...Nc6 and 4...c5, see Guarding b5: other fourth moves.}
5. Bd3 {Offers the trade, as against 3...Bf5.} Bxd3 {Almost half of Black players take. After 5...Bg6, push the h-pawn: 6.h4 e6 7.h5 Bxd3 8.cxd3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal), as on Key ideas: Plans against the bishop on f5.}
6. cxd3 e6 {Eight Black players in ten.}
7. Nf3 {[%cal Ga1c1,Gd1b3] Next come Rc1, Qb3 and castling. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Slav setup: 3...c6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{About one Black player in six plays 3...c6, as in the Slav Defense: the pawn guards d5, and the c8 bishop can still come out. After 4.e3, almost half bring it to f5. Offer the trade with Bd3, as against 3...Bf5. Black's other fourth moves are in Advanced variations.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c6
4. e3 Bf5 {Almost half of Black players. Against 4...e6, 4...Bg4 and 4...g6, see Slav setup: other fourth moves.}
5. Bd3 {Offers the trade.} Bxd3 {Half of Black players take. After 5...Bg6, push the h-pawn: 6.h4 e6 7.h5 Bxd3 8.cxd3 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).}
6. cxd3 e6
7. Nf3 {[%cal Gd1b3,Ga1c1] Qb3 hits b7, and Rc1 follows. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Pin on c3: 4.e3 and 4.Nf3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.e3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{Six White players in ten answer 3...e6 with 4.e3 or 4.Nf3. Pin the knight with ...Bb4: it is White's key piece, the one that wants to jump to b5. Then hit the center with ...c5. If White plays a3, take on c3: the doubled c-pawns are a target for ...Qa5. At this level, ...Bb4 is Black's best-scoring answer to both moves.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6
4. e3 {Half of White players.}
(4. Nf3 {One White player in eight.} Bb4 5. a3 {Almost four in ten. After 5.e3, play 5...c5: the position is the same as after 4.e3 Bb4 5.Nf3 c5.} Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.} 7. e3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] The queen hits the doubled pawn on c3. 8.Qd2? loses to 8...Ne4! (see Trap: 8.Qd2? and ...Ne4). Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black.})
4... Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Pins the knight on c3.}
5. Bd3 {Half of White players. Against 5.Ne2, 5.a3 and 5.Nf3, see Pin on c3: White's fifth moves.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 at once.}
6. dxc5 {Seven White players in ten. Against 6.a3, take on c3 and play ...Qa5 (see Pin on c3: White's sixth moves).} Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7c5] Develops and takes back on c5 next. Black scores 73% from here at this level.}
7. Ne2 {Four White players in ten.} Bxc5 {Takes back with the bishop. The knight stays on d7, ready for ...Nf6 or ...b6.}
8. O-O O-O {Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Check on b4: 4.Nb5 Bb4+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.Nb5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{One White player in four jumps to b5 at once, threatening Nxc7+. Answer with a check: after 5.c3 the bishop drops back to a5, and the pawn on c3 has taken the knight's way home. Then ...a6 hits the knight. Watch the a-pawn: after 6.a4, never retreat the bishop to b6, because a5 traps it.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6
4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Threatens Nxc7+.} Bb4+ {[%cal Gb4e1] Check first.}
5. c3 {Almost every White player.} Ba5 {[%cal Ga5e1] Keeps the bishop on the a5–e1 diagonal.}
6. a4 {[%cal Gb2b4] Three White players in ten. The pawn supports the knight on b5 and prepares b4, which hits the bishop. Against White's other sixth moves, see Check on b4: White's sixth moves.} a6 {[%cal Ga6b5] Hits the knight. 6...Bb6? loses the bishop to 7.a5 (see Trap to avoid: 6...Bb6? 7.a5).}
7. b4 {Eight White players in ten. Now the bishop is attacked.} Nc6! {[%cal Gc6b4] Develops and hits b4 a second time.}
8. bxa5 axb5 9. axb5 Nxa5 {Each side has taken a minor piece. Black's knight on a5 can jump to c4. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 4...Nc6? and Nb5!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5.Nb5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 3...c5 4.e3, one Black player in three develops with 4...Nc6?. It looks natural, but c7 now has only the queen to guard it. 5.Nb5! threatens Nc7+, forking king and rook, and White scores 69% from here at this level. The most common replies, 5...e5 and 5...Qa5+, both lose material.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5 4. e3 Nc6?
5. Nb5! {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Threatens Nc7+.} e5 {[%cal Ge5f4] Almost six Black players in ten hit the bishop.}
(5... Qa5+ {Four in ten check first.} 6. c3 e5 {Hits the bishop.} 7. Bxe5 {[%cal Gb5c7] Takes the pawn, and Nc7+ is still threatened.} Nxe5 8. dxe5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Now the knight on f6 is attacked as well. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White.})
6. dxe5 {[%cal Ge5f6,Gb5c7] Hits the knight on f6, and Nc7+ is still threatened.} Ne4 {Three Black players in ten.}
(6... a6 {A better try, chosen by one Black player in five. The best defense is 6...Qa5+ 7.Qd2 Qxd2+ 8.Kxd2, and White is still clearly better (Stockfish +1.6).} 7. Nc3 {[%cal Ge5f6] The knight goes back, and the knight on f6 is still attacked.} d4 8. exf6 dxc3 9. Qxd8+ Nxd8 10. b3 {White is a pawn up. Stockfish: +2.0, winning for White.})
(6... Nh5? 7. Qxh5 {The knight on h5 had no guard. Stockfish: +4.3, winning for White.})
7. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5e4,Gb5c7] Wins a pawn and hits the knight on e4. Nc7+ is still a threat.} Qa5+ 8. c3 a6 {Hits the knight.} 9. Nc7+ {The fork comes after all.} Qxc7 10. Qxe4 {White is a pawn up, with the better game. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5...Ne4? and Qxd5!"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "5...Ne4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 3...Nc6 4.Nb5 e5 5.dxe5, the knight on f6 is attacked. One Black player in six moves it to e4?, leaving the queen as the only guard of d5. Take the pawn: 6.Qxd5! hits the knight on e4, and 6...Qxd5 runs into the fork 7.Nxc7+. Most White players miss it: six in ten play 6.e6 instead. Taking on e5 with the knight is worse still: 5...Nxe5?? 6.Bxe5 wins a piece.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Nc6 4. Nb5 e5 5. dxe5 Ne4?
(5... Nxe5?? {One Black player in fifteen. The knight on e5 has no guard.} 6. Bxe5 {White is a piece up, and Nxc7+ is still threatened. Stockfish: +5.3, winning for White.})
6. Qxd5! {[%cal Gd5e4,Gb5c7] Takes the pawn and hits the knight.} Bf5 {Guards the knight.}
(6... Qxd5 {Takes the queen, but the knight has a fork.} 7. Nxc7+ {[%cal Gc7e8,Gc7d5] Check, and the queen on d5 is hit.} Kd8 8. Nxd5 {White is two pawns up. Stockfish: +3.5, winning for White.})
7. Qxd8+ Rxd8 8. f3 {[%cal Gf3e4] Drives the knight away. Nxc7+ is still a threat. Stockfish: +3.1, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 6...h5? and Bxg6"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...h5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{Against h4, Black sometimes stops the pawn with ...h5?. But then the h-pawn no longer guards g6. After Bxg6 fxg6, the pawn on g6 has no guard, and Qd3 hits it. The same trap works after 3...a6 4.e3 Bf5 5.Bd3 Bg6 6.h4 h5?. White scores 94% after 6...h5 at this level, in a small number of games.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Bd3 Bg6 6. h4 h5?
7. Bxg6 {[%cal Gd3g6] Takes the bishop.} fxg6 {[%csl Yg6] The h-pawn is on h5, so the f-pawn must take back.}
8. Qd3 {[%cal Gd3g6] Hits g6. Qxg6+ comes next.} Bd6 9. Qxg6+ Kf8 {White is a pawn up, and Black's king can't castle. Stockfish: +2.9, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 8.Qd2? and ...Ne4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "8.Qd2"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.e3 Bb4 5.a3 Bxc3+ 6.bxc3 c5 7.Nf3 Qa5, three White players in ten guard c3 with 8.Qd2?. 8...Ne4! hits the queen, and when it moves, c3 falls. Black scores 89% after 8.Qd2 at this level. The same trick works one move earlier: 5.Qd2? Ne4! hits the queen, and the knight on c3 is pinned.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bb4 5. a3
(5. Qd2? {Guards c3, but the queen is exposed.} Ne4! {[%cal Ge4d2,Ge4c3] Hits the queen, and the knight on c3 is pinned.} 6. Qd3 {The best square.} Ba5 {[%cal Ge4c3] Black is better developed, and the knight on c3 is still under fire. Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black.})
5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3]}
8. Qd2? {Guards c3 with the queen.} Ne4! {[%cal Ge4d2] Hits the queen.}
9. Qd3 {Seven White players in ten. After 9.Qc1 or 9.Qd1, take the pawn at once: 9...Qxc3+ (Stockfish −4.1 and −4.7, winning for Black).} c4! {[%cal Gc4d3] Drives the queen away from c3.}
10. Qd1 Qxc3+ {[%cal Gc3a1] Check, and the rook on a1 is hit. Black is a pawn up, and White's king can't castle. Stockfish: −3.7, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid: 6...Bb6? 7.a5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.a4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.Nb5 Bb4+ 5.c3 Ba5 6.a4, the bishop on a5 is short of squares. If it drops back to b6, the a-pawn traps it: 7.a5 hits it, and its only safe squares are taken. One Black player in twenty-five plays 6...Bb6?, and White scores 91% from there. Play 6...a6!, which hits the knight.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Bb4+ 5. c3 Ba5 6. a4 a6!
(6... Bb6? {Steps off the a-file.} 7. a5 {[%cal Ga5b6] Hits the bishop. On a7 or c5 it would be taken.} Bxa5 8. Rxa5 {White has won a bishop for a pawn. Stockfish: +4.5, winning for White.})
7. Na3 {[%csl Ya3] The knight has to go to the edge. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Pin on c3: White's fifth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "5.Ne2"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.e3 Bb4, half of White players develop with 5.Bd3 (see Pin on c3: 4.e3 and 4.Nf3). Against 5.Ne2, castle, and drop the bishop back to e7 when a3 asks it. Against 5.a3, take on c3 and hit the doubled pawn with ...c5 and ...Qa5. Against 5.Nf3, hit d4 with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bb4
5. Ne2 {One White player in six. The knight is ready to take back on c3, so the pin does less.}
(5. a3 {One in seven.} Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 {Black scores 77% from here at this level.} 7. Nf3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] The queen hits the doubled pawn on c3. 8.Qd2? loses to 8...Ne4! (see Trap: 8.Qd2? and ...Ne4). Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black.})
(5. Nf3 {One in twelve.} c5 6. dxc5 {Three White players in ten. Against 6.a3, take on c3: 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 Qa5 (Stockfish −1.2, clearly better for Black).} Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7c5] Takes back on c5 with the knight next. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.})
5... O-O 6. a3 {Eight White players in ten.} Be7 {[%cal Gc7c5] The bishop drops back, and ...c5 comes next. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Pin on c3: White's sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.a3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.e3 Bb4 5.Bd3 c5, about one White player in seven asks the bishop with 6.a3. Take on c3 and hit the doubled pawn with ...Qa5, as against 5.a3. Black scores 72% after 6...Bxc3+ at this level. After 6.dxc5 Nbd7 7.Nf3, take back with the knight.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. e3 Bb4 5. Bd3 c5
6. a3 {One White player in seven.}
(6. dxc5 Nbd7 7. Nf3 {One White player in four.} Nxc5 {[%cal Gc5d3] Takes back and hits the bishop on d3. Stockfish: −0.3, slightly better for Black.})
6... Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Hits c3. Stockfish: −1.0, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Check on b4: White's sixth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.b4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.Nb5 Bb4+ 5.c3 Ba5, White's sixth moves split four ways. Against 6.b4, three White players in ten, drop back to b6, and meet 7.a4 with 7...a5!: the natural 7...a6? loses the bishop to 8.a5. Against 6.e3 and 6.Nf3, castle. Against 6.Qa4, which hits the bishop, guard it with 6...Nc6. After 6.a4 a6 7.Na3, hit the center with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Bb4+ 5. c3 Ba5
6. b4 {Three White players in ten.}
(6. e3 {One in five.} O-O 7. Nf3 {Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
(6. Qa4 {One in ten. The queen hits the bishop on a5.} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6a5] The knight guards the bishop. Black scores 61% from here at this level. Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.})
6... Bb6 {[%cal Gb6f2] The bishop drops back while White's a-pawn is still on a2.}
7. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Nine White players in ten. White threatens a5, trapping the bishop.} a5! {[%cal Ga5b4] Stops a5 for good and hits b4. Black scores 70% from here at this level.}
(7... a6? {More than half of Black players. It looks natural, but the knight on b5 covers a7.} 8. a5 {[%cal Ga5b6,Gb5a7] Hits the bishop: on a7 the knight would take it. Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White.})
8. e3 {Two White players in three.} axb4 9. cxb4 {Stockfish: −0.6, slightly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Knight to b5: 4...Bd6 and 4...Bb4+"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...Bd6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.Nb5, three Black players in ten offer to trade the f4 bishop with 4...Bd6. Take the bishop with the knight: White gets the bishop pair, and Black's doubled d-pawns hem in Black's own pieces. One Black player in twenty checks with 4...Bb4+, the Black repertoire's move in this course: answer 5.c3, and after 5...Ba5 6.a4 a6, drop the knight back to a3 (see Check on b4: 4.Nb5 Bb4+).}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Bd6 {[%cal Gd6f4]}
5. Nxd6+ {Take the bishop. White gets the bishop pair.} cxd6 6. e3 O-O {Against 6...Nc6, almost as common, play 7.Nf3 just the same.}
7. Nf3 {[%cal Gf1d3,Gc2c3] Next come Bd3, c3 and castling. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Knight to b5: other fifth and sixth moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...Bd6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 4.Nb5 Na6 5.e3 c6 6.Nc3, about four Black players in ten develop the f8 bishop before bringing the knight back with ...Nc7. Then the knight on a6 has only the b7 pawn to guard it. Take it: Bxa6 bxa6 leaves Black with doubled a-pawns for the rest of the game. Against 5...Be7 or 5...Bd7, keep developing with Nf3 or c3.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6 4. Nb5 Na6 5. e3 c6
6. Nc3 Bd6 {About one Black player in four.}
7. Bxa6 {Takes the knight before it can go back.} bxa6 8. Na4 {[%cal Ga4c5] The knight heads for c5, the hole in front of the c6 pawn. Stockfish: +0.6, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "White's other fourth moves"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.a3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{About one White player in eleven plays 4.a3, which stops ...Bb4. Hit the center with ...c5 instead. Rarer fourth moves, 4.f3, 4.Qd2 and 4.Qd3, are answered the same way: 4...c5, and Black is slightly better in each.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 e6
4. a3 {One White player in eleven.}
(4. f3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4. Stockfish: −0.9, slightly better for Black.})
4... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
5. e3 {Two White players in three.} a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5 and keeps White's knight off b5. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Guarding b5: other fourth moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...e6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 3...a6 4.e3, Black's moves other than 4...Bf5 call for the same London setup: Bd3, Nf3 and castling. Against 4...e6 5.Bd3 c5, take on c5 and develop. Against 4...Nc6, develop the same way, and chase ...Bg4 with f3 and g4. Against 4...c5, take on c5 and prepare b4 to hold the pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 a6 4. e3 e6 {Almost three Black players in ten.}
(4... Nc6 {One Black player in seven.} 5. Bd3 e6 6. Nf3 {[%cal Ge1g1] Castle next. Against 5...Bg4, play 6.f3 and chase the bishop with g4. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.})
5. Bd3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Almost four in ten hit the center.}
6. dxc5 {Six White players in ten take. White gives up the center for quick development.} Bxc5 7. Nf3 {Castling and e4 come next. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Hitting d4: 3...c5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{About one Black player in eleven hits d4 at once with 3...c5. Play 4.e3, and keep the knight ready for b5. Against 4...cxd4, take back with the e-pawn: the e-file opens, and the f4 bishop stays active. Against 4...e6, jump to b5 at once. Against 4...Nc6?, see Trap: 4...Nc6? and Nb5!.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c5
4. e3 cxd4 {One Black player in five.}
5. exd4 {[%cal Ge1e8] The e-file opens.} a6 {Four Black players in ten. It takes b5 away from White's knight.}
6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5c6] Hits the knight on c6. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Slav setup: other fourth moves"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...e6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 3...c6 4.e3, Black's main alternatives to 4...Bf5 each have a clear answer. Against 4...e6, develop with Bd3 and Nf3. Against 4...Bg4, chase the bishop with f3, g4 and h4 (see Key ideas: Plans against the bishop on f5). Against 4...g6, push the h-pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 c6 4. e3 e6 {One Black player in six.}
5. Bd3 Bd6 {Half of Black players offer the trade.}
6. Nf3 {[%cal Gf4d6] If Black takes on f4, take back with the e-pawn: it controls e5, and the e-file opens for your rook. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Knight to c6: 3...Nc6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{One Black player in fifteen plays 3...Nc6, which leaves c7 unguarded. Jump to b5 at once: nine Black players in ten answer 4...e5, giving a pawn to hit the bishop. Take on e5 with the pawn. White scores 64% after 4.Nb5 at this level. Against 5...Ne4? and 5...Nxe5??, see Trap: 5...Ne4? and Qxd5!.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Nc6
4. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7,Gf4c7] Threatens Nxc7+.} e5 {Hits the bishop and opens the center.}
5. dxe5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Takes the pawn and hits the knight on f6.} Nh5 {Six Black players in ten. The knight attacks the bishop on f4.}
6. e3 {Lets the bishop be taken.} Nxf4 7. exf4 {White is a pawn up, and the doubled f-pawns guard e5. Stockfish: +0.9, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Fianchetto: 3...g6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{One Black player in twenty fianchettoes the f8 bishop. Develop with e3, then push the h-pawn: h4 and h5 aim at the g6 pawn and open the h-file. Three White players in ten choose 5.h4 at this level, and it scores best.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 g6
4. e3 Bg7 {Eight Black players in ten.}
5. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The h-pawn heads for h5.} O-O
6. h5 {[%cal Gh5g6] Hits g6. If Black takes on h5, the h-file opens.} c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Black hits back in the center.}
7. hxg6 hxg6 {[%cal Gh1h8] The h-file is open for White's rook. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Early bishop: 5...Bg6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System"]
[ECO "D01"]

{After 3...Bf5 4.e3 e6 5.Bd3, three Black players in ten keep the bishop with 5...Bg6. Push the h-pawn: h4 threatens h5, which drives the bishop off the diagonal, and Black usually trades on d3 after all. Never fear 6...h5?: it loses a pawn (see Trap: 6...h5? and Bxg6).}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Bf4 Bf5 4. e3 e6 5. Bd3 Bg6
6. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Threatens h5.} Bd6 {[%cal Gd6f4] The most common reply: Black offers a trade of the dark-squared bishops.}
7. h5 {Gains space and hits the bishop.} Bxd3 8. cxd3 {Take back with the c-pawn, as usual. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Move orders: 2...e6"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "2...e6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Rapport-Jobava System, with e6"]
[ECO "D01"]

{About one Black player in five answers 2.Nc3 with 2...e6, keeping the knight on g8 for now. Play 3.Bf4 all the same. After 3...Nf6 you are in Knight to b5: 3...e6 4.Nb5, and after 3...a6 4.e3 Nf6 in Guarding b5: 3...a6. Against an early ...Bd6, play e3 and let Black take on f4: the pawn that takes back controls e5. Against an early ...Bb4, guard c3 with the queen.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 e6
3. Bf4 Bd6 {One Black player in six offers the trade of bishops at once.}
4. e3 {[%cal Gf4d6] Lets Black take on f4.} Bxf4 {Two Black players in three take at once. After 4...Nf6, the position is the same as after 3...e6 4.e3 Bd6: develop with Bd3 and Nf3.}
5. exf4 {[%cal Ge1e8,Gf4e5] The pawn on f4 controls e5, and the e-file opens for a rook.} Nf6 {More than half of Black players.}
6. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3e5] The knight heads for e5. Bd3 and castling come next. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Move orders: 2...c6 and 2...Bf5"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "2...c6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Queen's Pawn Game: Chigorin Variation"]
[ECO "D00"]

{One Black player in nine answers 2.Nc3 with 2...c6, and about one in eleven with 2...Bf5. Play 3.Bf4 and 4.e3. Most games then transpose to a chapter of this course once Black plays ...Nf6: to Slav setup: 3...c6 or Early bishop: 3...Bf5. Against ...Bf5, the plan is the same as always: Bd3 to trade the bishop.}
1. d4 d5 2. Nc3 c6
(2... Bf5 3. Bf4 e6 {Half of Black players.} 4. e3 {After 4...Nf6, the position is the same as in Early bishop: 3...Bf5 after 4...e6.} Nf6 5. Bd3 {Offers the trade, as in that chapter. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.})
3. Bf4 Bf5 {A third of Black players. After 3...Nf6 4.e3, you are in Slav setup: 3...c6.}
4. e3 e6 5. Bd3 {[%cal Gd3f5] Offers the trade. Stockfish: 0.0, equal.} *

[Chapter "Move orders: 1...Nf6 2.Nc3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "2...g6"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Indian Defense"]
[ECO "A45"]

{After 1.d4 Nf6 2.Nc3, almost four Black players in ten play 2...d5, and 3.Bf4 reaches the Jobava. One in four plays 2...g6, and one in four 2...e6. Play 3.Bf4 against both. Against the King's Indian setup with ...g6, ...Bg7 and ...d6, develop calmly with e3, Nf3 and h3. Against 2...e6 3.Bf4 Bb4, guard the knight with the queen.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6
(2... e6 3. Bf4 Bb4 {One Black player in four. After 3...d5 you are in Knight to b5: 3...e6 4.Nb5.} 4. Qd3 {[%cal Gd3c3] Guards the knight and prepares a3 or e4. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.})
3. Bf4 {[%engine ok] Stockfish prefers 3.e4, which leads to a Pirc Defense. 3.Bf4 keeps the Jobava setup, and one White player in five chooses it at this level.} Bg7 {Three Black players in four. After 3...d5 4.e3 Bg7, you are in Fianchetto: 3...g6.}
4. e3 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. h3 {[%cal Gf4h2] Keeps a square on h2 for the bishop, so ...Nh5 doesn't win it. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

